# The Tcl/Tk toolchain the Tk front end links against.  The sources under
# tcltk/tcl9.0.4 and tcltk/tk9.0.4 are vendored and tracked, because CI cannot
# see archive/ and a front end whose toolchain lives only on one machine is not
# buildable anywhere else.  What scripts/build-tcltk produces from them is not:
# tcltk/build is the out-of-tree object tree and tcltk/local is the install
# prefix, both reproducible in about two minutes.
tcltk/build/
tcltk/local/

# And four rules above have to be undone inside it.  This repository ignores
# `configure`, `aclocal.m4`, `*.exe` and `*.map` because for *our* source tree
# they are build artefacts -- but in an upstream release tarball they are
# shipped files, and Tcl's and Tk's `unix/configure` are the very things
# scripts/build-tcltk runs first.  Without these exceptions the vendored tree
# commits with fourteen files missing, every clone that is not this Mac fails
# at the first configure, and nothing says why.
!tcltk/**/configure
!tcltk/**/aclocal.m4
!tcltk/**/*.exe
!tcltk/**/*.map

# Vendoring an upstream release means carrying its weight, so three parts of
# it are trimmed rather than committed.  They are ignored as well as deleted
# because the next person to unpack a Tcl or Tk tarball over this directory --
# to move to 9.0.5, say -- would otherwise silently add all three back, and
# 36MB is not the kind of thing anyone notices in a diff.
#
#   pkgs/       the bundled thread, sqlite, itcl and tdbc packages.  We build
#               `binaries libraries` rather than `all` precisely so this step
#               never runs, both because we need none of the four and because
#               the two bootstrap traps in section 2.1 of the Phase 3 plan
#               live in it.  Verified: the toolchain builds without it.
#   tests/      Tcl's and Tk's own suites, for `make test`, which we do not
#               run.  Our tests are in src/tests and, from T0, tcltk-driven.
#   doc/        nroff man pages, installed by `install-doc`, which we do not
#               call.  The Tcl and Tk documentation is online.
#
# Everything else stays, including compat/, win/ and xlib/ -- decision 13 in
# the Phase 3 plan makes Windows and Linux deliverables, so the platform trees
# are not ours to trim even while macOS is the only one being built.
tcltk/*/pkgs/
tcltk/*/tests/
tcltk/*/tests-perf/
tcltk/*/doc/

# Emscripten object trees; the wasm artefacts land in build-wasm/, matched by
# the build*/ rule above.
src/wasm-obj/
src/wasm-obj-test/
build-asan/

# Makefile.osx-tty's object tree, and the tarball it packages.  The objects
# get their own directory rather than going in src/ beside everything else
# because the Cocoa build compiles the same files with -DMACH_O_CARBON and the
# two must not overwrite each other's .o files; see the header of
# src/Makefile.osx-tty.  The tarball is matched by name because *.tar.gz is
# not ignored wholesale -- the source archive is built at the top level too.
src/osx-tty/
*-osx-terminal.tar.gz

# The Neon tileset writes two pictures for inspection -- a mock map and a
# filmstrip of the colour cycle.  They live beside the source art rather than
# beside the sheet, because Makefile.osx copies lib/tiles wholesale into the
# app bundle and these have no business shipping.
scripts/tiles/neon/preview.png
scripts/tiles/neon/preview-actual-size.png
scripts/tiles/neon/cycle.png